  "_id": "6a7f5797757ee51246381c4e",
  "lab": "6a7f5796eda1a5feaff0b458",
  "name": "Cortisol AM",
  "code": "CORT AM",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>Cortisol Level (mcg/dL or nmol/L)</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Morning (AM) Cortisol Levels</div></td><td><div>Normally, cortisol levels are higher in the morning, shortly after waking up. This is known as the \"morning cortisol surge\" and helps to provide energy for the day's activities. Morning cortisol levels in the range of 6 - 23 mcg/dL (or 166 - 635 nmol/L) are considered within the normal range.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Evening (PM) Cortisol Levels</div></td><td><div>Cortisol levels gradually decrease throughout the day, reaching their lowest point in the evening. Evening cortisol levels in the range of 2 - 11 mcg/dL (or 55 - 305 nmol/L) are considered within the normal range.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Abnormal Cortisol Levels</div></td><td><div>Abnormal cortisol levels can indicate various medical conditions. High cortisol levels, known as hypercortisolism or Cushing's syndrome, may be caused by conditions such as adrenal tumors, pituitary tumors, or chronic stress. Low cortisol levels, known as hypocortisolism or adrenal insufficiency, may be caused by conditions affecting the adrenal glands, such as Addison's disease. Further evaluation and testing are required to determine the underlying cause of abnormal cortisol levels.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
